5
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.360
So subcutaneous is a novel
by Aaron Reed that came out in 2020.

6
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.000
Many of you might already be familiar
with Aaron, read his work,

7
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:24.160
and maybe this novel itself,
which he actually spoke about at the 2020

8
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.280
ello conference,
which was also hosted by Florida

9
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.080
and online, wasn't supposed to be online
that year,

10
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:37.480
but subcutaneous is a queer
coming of age novel.

11
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:41.120
The narrator is an introverted
college student

12
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.520
whose love for his closest
friend, Nico, goes unrequited.

13
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.600
Subcutaneous is also a horror novel.

14
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.760
Ryan and Nico discover a vast basement
under their dilapidated off campus

15
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.600
house that branches into parallel worlds,
which they get lost in.

16
00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.120
And most importantly for today,
subcutaneous is an experimental novel.

17
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.240
It's human written

18
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.280
but procedurally generated,
and no copy is the same as any other copy.

19
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.240
So the printed book, like what
I have here, or if you have an e-book

20
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.240
somewhere, it will read as a singular,

21
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.480
cohesive, linear textual object.

22
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.480
Nothing inside indicates
that the novel is variable,

23
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.520
that every copy of subcutaneous
is different.

24
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.240
So Aaron actually gave the keynote
at that 2020

25
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.040
Yellow Conference, and in that keynote

26
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.880
he explained how subcutaneous
worked at a kind of high level.

27
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.920
This is directly from Aaron.

28
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.720
Each time a book is ordered,
a different version of the text

29
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.280
is rendered from a source text
containing many possible variations

30
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:47.560
of individual words, sentences,
and even entire scenes all come together.

31
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:48.480
And then there are

32
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.400
thousands, actually hundreds of thousands
of possible variations.

33
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.480
And at any given moment,
any reader only has access

34
00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.480
to the ones
that are in their copy of the book.

35
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:05.040
Also at the 2020 conference,
Andrew Ferguson gave a talk

36
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.320
